Fixed-Rate Hedging Strategies
  Forward Hedge
    The Forward Hedge serves as our benchmark for total certainty, locking in proceeds at approximately $4,855,500 based on the current forward rate. This strategy provides 100% visibility into future cash flows, effectively eliminating the "downside tail risk" associated with a weakening Euro. While it offers the highest level of budget stability, it comes with an opportunity cost: the firm will not benefit from any favorable Euro appreciation beyond the locked-in rate.
  Money Market Hedge
    Operating as a Synthetic Parity to the forward contract, the Money Market Hedge yields net proceeds of approximately $4,853,200. Because this strategy relies on Interest Rate Parity—borrowing Euros today, converting them at the spot rate, and investing the proceeds in USD—the outcome is nearly identical to the forward hedge. The primary drawback is operational: this method consumes the firm's Euro borrowing capacity and liquidity, which may be better reserved for day-to-day solar equipment import operations.

Optionality and Market Participation
  Currency Put Option
    For a more flexible "insurance" approach, the Put Option establishes a floor for our proceeds at a minimum of $4,770,000 (after accounting for the upfront premium). This strategy allows the firm to protect against a Euro collapse while keeping the "upside" open; if the Euro rallies, we simply let the option expire and convert at the higher spot rate. It is strategically ideal if the treasury team expects significant Euro volatility but wants to retain the potential for higher USD returns.
  Call Option
    While available in the market, a Call Option is generally irrelevant for managing a receivable, as it provides the right to buy currency rather than sell it. Traditionally, this instrument is reserved for hedging payables or utilized within a complex "collar" strategy to offset the costs of a put premium. For our current €4,500,000 receivable, it does not offer direct protection.

The Baseline of Inaction
  No Hedge (Benchmark)
    Maintaining a No Hedge position serves as our baseline risk benchmark. While it carries no upfront costs, it exposes the firm to the full force of market volatility. For instance, if the Euro were to drop by just 5%, the firm would lose over $240,000 in value compared to the current spot rate. This "do-nothing" approach is high-risk and serves primarily to quantify the value added by our active hedging strategies.
